insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at)
values
  ('the-second-year-after-a-death', 'The Second Year After a Death', 'The casseroles stopped, the cards stopped, and everyone assumes you are through it. Why the second year is often harder than the first, and what helps in it.', 'The first year had markers in it. The funeral, the paperwork, the first birthday, the first Christmas, people checking on you. Everyone knew.

Now it is eighteen months and nobody mentions it. You are expected to be fine, and you are in some ways less fine than you were, and there is nowhere to put that.

Why the second year can be worse

Several things change at once, and none of them get talked about.

The adrenaline goes. The first months are often spent in a kind of emergency state: arrangements, administration, people in the house. That carries you, badly, and when it drains away you are left with the actual absence.

The support thins out. It is not that people stopped caring; it is that a death moves out of the news, and they assume someone else is checking in, or that mentioning it would upset you.

And the permanence lands. In the first year some part of you is still half expecting them back. In the second, you have lived through a whole cycle of a year without them, and you know what every season looks like now.

That is why people often say the second year is quieter and heavier, and why so many of them think something has gone wrong with them.

Nothing has gone wrong with you

Grief is not a ladder you climb. It comes in waves that get further apart and can stay just as high.

So a Tuesday in the eighteenth month can be as bad as week two, set off by a smell, a song, an anniversary you had not thought about, or by nothing. That is normal and it does not mean you are going backward.

Expect it to keep showing up in new places. The first time you need their advice. The first family event where their chair is filled by someone else. The moment you notice you have forgotten the sound of their voice, which frightens people more than almost anything.

Say it out loud, to someone

The silence around the second year is the part you can actually change.

Most people around you have simply assumed the subject is closed. Telling one or two of them plainly — this year is harder than last year, I still want to talk about him — usually produces more than you expect, because they were waiting for a signal.

Say their name in ordinary conversation. Keep telling the stories. The pressure to stop referring to someone is almost entirely imagined, and the people who loved them usually want to hear it.

If talking to family is complicated, a bereavement group is a reasonable alternative. Everyone there is in the same position and nobody needs it explained.

Rebuild the week deliberately

A large part of the second year is practical, and it is nobody''s favorite subject: the structure that the person provided is gone and has to be replaced on purpose.

If they were your company in the evenings, the evenings need something in them. If they organized the social life, you now do that, badly at first. If they were the one who cooked, or drove, or handled the money, those are skills to pick up rather than failures of character.

Start with two fixed things a week that involve other people, and treat them as appointments. Volunteering, a class, a regular walk, a standing phone call. Not as a replacement, which is impossible, but as a floor to stand on.

And be careful with the house. Sitting alone in the same rooms for months is the thing most likely to turn grief into something heavier, and leaving once a day is a small, dull, effective intervention.

Let the relationship change rather than end

People often feel they must choose between holding on and moving on. In practice most people find a third thing.

Keep a place for them: the photograph, the ritual on the date, the recipe, the garden, the charity, telling their jokes. Some people write to them, or talk to them in the car, and find it steadying rather than strange.

And let your life grow around that rather than instead of it. Enjoying something is not a betrayal, and neither is a good week. Guilt about laughing is extremely common and it is not information about your loyalty.

Be ready for the second-year decisions

This is often when the big choices arrive: the house, the move, the relationship, the job, what to do with the money.

Where you can, give them time. Decisions made in the first year are often made by somebody in shock; the second year is better, and it is still worth talking each one through with somebody who is not inside it.

And expect other people to have opinions about the pace of your life, particularly about dating, selling, or clearing things out. That pace is yours.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if you are no further on than you were a year ago, if you cannot think about anything else, if you have been unable to work or look after yourself, or if you are drinking more to get through the evenings.

Grief does not have a schedule, but it should be changing shape over time; when it stays frozen, there is specific help for that, and bereavement services will often see you without a referral, two years on or ten. Say how long it has been and what you cannot do at the moment, because that is what tells them which kind of support fits.

And if it has taken you somewhere dark, and you are not sure you want to be here, please tell a doctor or someone you trust today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Grief', 'Life Transitions']::text[], 0, '2026-10-15T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-you-are-sleeping-far-too-much', 'When You Are Sleeping Far Too Much', 'Eleven hours and still exhausted, naps that eat the afternoon, a bed that is the only comfortable place. Why sleeping too much makes you feel worse, and how to pull the day back.', 'You slept from ten until nine, went back to bed at two, and you are still tired. Not sleepy exactly, but heavy, like you are moving through something thicker than air.

Nobody worries about this the way they worry about insomnia, so people tend to carry it quietly and assume it is laziness. It is usually not.

Sleeping more and resting less

The strange part is how little of it feels restorative.

Long sleep with a low mood is often shallow and broken up, so twelve hours can leave you less rested than seven. And the more of the day you spend in bed, the weaker your body clock''s signal becomes, which makes the next night worse, which makes the next day heavier.

Bed also becomes the only place that feels tolerable, which is understandable and is a trap: it removes daylight, movement and other people, which are the three things most likely to lift the heaviness a little.

That is the loop to interrupt, and you interrupt it from the daytime end rather than the night end.

Get checked, because several physical things do this

Before treating it as mood alone, see a doctor and say plainly how much you are sleeping.

Thyroid trouble, anemia, low vitamin D, diabetes, long covid and sleep apnea all produce exactly this, and apnea in particular is widely missed. If you snore heavily, wake with a dry mouth or a headache, or someone has seen you stop breathing, say so, because that is treatable and nothing else works until it is.

Medications matter too. Some antidepressants, antihistamines, painkillers and blood pressure tablets are sedating, and the fix is often a change of dose or timing rather than enduring it.

And alcohol is worth an honest look: a drink in the evening reliably produces long, poor-quality sleep.

Fix the getting-up end, not the going-to-bed end

This is the single most effective change, and it is the least appealing.

Pick one get-up time and keep it every day, including weekends, including after a bad night. It does not have to be early; it has to be the same. Everything else follows from that anchor.

Then get light into your eyes within half an hour. Outside is far better than indoors, even in gray weather, even for five minutes on the step. Light is the main signal your body clock uses, and a dark morning in bed tells it there is no reason to start the day.

Have something small and fixed to get up for: the kettle, the dog, the radio at a certain time, a shower, stepping outside. A reason you do not have to invent at eight in the morning.

Make the bed less available in the daytime

You do not have to be heroic about this. You have to make lying down slightly harder.

Get dressed, even into different soft clothes. Make the bed, which sounds like a parent talking and genuinely reduces how often people get back in. Sit in another room, even if you do the same nothing there.

If you nap, make it deliberate rather than accidental: twenty minutes, before mid-afternoon, with an alarm, on top of the covers. A two-hour afternoon sleep is the main reason the evening stretches and the night falls apart.

Put something in the first hour

An empty day pulls you back to bed by lunchtime, so give the morning one small thing with a time attached.

A walk to the end of the road. A shower. One chore. One message to a person. A class, a shift, an errand. Not a productive day; one anchor, which makes the next hour easier to be upright in.

And move your body a little, earlier rather than later. Even a slow ten minutes outside does more for the heaviness than another hour horizontal, which is maddening when you are exhausted and is nonetheless true.

Expect the energy to arrive after the activity rather than before it. Waiting to feel up to something is how weeks disappear.

Be careful how you talk about it

People in this state are usually much harder on themselves than anyone else is.

Lazy, pathetic, wasting my life. Those sentences are the heaviness talking, and they reliably make the next morning worse rather than earlier.

Something flatter is more useful: I am sleeping too much because something is wrong, and the way out is one get-up time and some daylight. Then do the small version and count it.

When to get help

Please talk to a doctor if this has gone on for more than a couple of weeks, if you are sleeping through commitments, if your mood is low alongside it, or if you have lost interest in things that used to matter.

Sleeping far too much is one of the clearest signs that something treatable is going on, whether that turns out to be physical, a medication, or a low mood, and it is worth saying out loud rather than apologizing for. Ask for your bloods to be checked and for a sleep assessment if the snoring or the daytime heaviness fits; ask too about treatment for the mood itself, which responds well, and a therapist can help you rebuild the structure of a day while that works.

And if things have become dark enough that you are not sure you want to be here, please tell a doctor or someone you trust today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Depression', 'Habits']::text[], 0, '2026-10-15T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('deciding-what-to-eat-every-single-day', 'Deciding What to Eat Every Single Day', 'Three meals a day, every day, forever, and the deciding is the exhausting part. How to take most of that decision off your plate without turning it into a project.', 'It is half past five and the question arrives again. What are we eating. You open the fridge, look at the same shelves you looked at this morning, close it, and twenty minutes later you are ordering something or eating toast standing up.

The cooking is usually not the hard part. The deciding is.

Why this one decision wears people down

It is relentless, it is low-reward, and it comes at the worst time of day.

Most households face it two or three times daily, which is something like a thousand decisions a year, each with constraints: what is in, what will keep, who is in, what people will actually eat, how long it takes, what it costs.

And it lands at the hour when you have least left. By six in the evening, after a day of other decisions, nobody is at their best, which is why the answer is so often nothing or takeout.

That is not a willpower failure. It is a badly designed system, and systems can be changed.

Decide once rather than daily

The single biggest change is moving the decision away from the moment of hunger.

Pick a short, boring list of meals you can actually make — eight or ten is plenty — and rotate it. People resist this because it sounds joyless, and in practice it removes most of the daily grind while you still eat things you like.

Give the week a shape if that helps: something with pasta, something with eggs, something from the freezer, fish, a roast, leftovers, one takeout. Then the only question each day is which of those, which is a much smaller question than what is dinner.

And write the week''s meals somewhere visible. Not a plan to live up to; a note so that nobody has to decide at half past five.

Shop from the list, not from the shop

Most evening paralysis is really a shopping problem, because a fridge of unrelated items offers no meals at all.

So shop for the meals you wrote down, which also cuts waste and spending. Keep a standing list of the basics you always want in, and buy those without thinking.

Then keep a shelf of emergency meals you do not have to decide about: tinned tomatoes and pasta, eggs, frozen vegetables, a bag of rice, something in the freezer you made double of. On the worst days, that shelf is the difference between a meal and nothing.

Cook once, eat twice

Deciding is expensive; reheating is free.

Make double of anything that reheats and treat the second portion as a meal rather than as a failure. Freeze single servings, which are far more useful than a large dish you need to plan around.

If you have one hour at the weekend, spend it on components rather than finished meals: a pot of rice, roasted vegetables, cooked chicken, a sauce. Then weeknight cooking becomes assembly, which takes a fraction of the thought.

Lower the standard on purpose

A meal does not have to be a composed plate. Toast with something on it is a meal. Eggs and a tomato is a meal. Beans, cheese, a baked potato is a meal.

Letting a few meals a week be that simple is what makes the other nights possible, and it is a great deal better than the thing that actually happens when the bar is too high, which is eating nothing much at nine o''clock.

If you are cooking for other people, be plain about it too: tonight is a simple one. Most families mind far less than the cook imagines.

Share the deciding, or let someone else do it

If you live with other adults, the deciding is a task like any other and it can move.

One person picks the week, another shops, the children choose one night each, or whoever is home first starts whatever was written down. The point is that it stops being one person''s invisible job.

And where money allows, let a service take some of it: a delivery slot, a subscription box, a recurring order of the same basics. Paying to remove a decision is a reasonable use of money, not a luxury.

When eating is harder than organizing

Some of this is not really about planning.

If you are not eating because you have no appetite, because cooking is beyond you at the moment, or because food has become loaded for you, those need something gentler than a meal plan. Eat whatever is easiest and repeatable, prioritize having something rather than something good, and accept toast for a while.

And look at your circumstances honestly. Shift work, caring for someone, no money, no energy, a small kitchen: those are real constraints, not excuses, and the plan has to fit the life you actually have.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a doctor if you have stopped eating properly for more than a couple of weeks, if you are losing or gaining weight without meaning to, if cooking and eating have become impossible rather than tedious, or if your appetite has gone along with a low mood.

Speak to a doctor or a therapist too if food has become a source of real anxiety, control or shame rather than a daily chore; those patterns respond well to proper help and deserve more than another system. And if money is the obstacle, ask a professional at your doctor''s office or a local advice service what is available, because food support exists in most places and is widely unclaimed.', array['Food', 'Decision Making']::text[], 0, '2026-10-15T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('the-shoulds-you-carry-around', 'The Shoulds You Carry Around', 'I should be further on. I should not need this much rest. Why rules that sound like standards tend to produce guilt rather than action, and how to turn one into something usable.', 'I should be over this by now. I should not need a nap at four in the afternoon. I should be able to handle my own mother. I should have a house by thirty-five.

Each of those sounds like a standard. Notice what they actually produce: not action, but a dull background guilt that follows you around.

What a should is really doing

A should is a rule you are holding yourself to, usually one you never consciously agreed to.

Most of them were installed rather than chosen: from a parent, a school, a religion, a job, a decade of advertising. And because they feel like obvious truths rather than opinions, they do not get examined the way a decision would.

The tell is the feeling that follows. A useful intention produces a plan. A should produces guilt, and guilt with nowhere to go turns into either paralysis or resentment.

The same rule aimed outward is just as costly: they should have called, he should know better, people should not behave like that. Those produce anger rather than guilt, and they tend to keep you stuck arguing with how the world is.

Say it out loud and listen to it

Take the sentence and say it in full, in your own voice: I should be able to visit my father every week without getting upset.

Said out loud, most shoulds sound less like a law and more like something a particular person once said. That is the point. The rules lose a surprising amount of power when heard rather than assumed.

Then ask three plain questions. Who says? Does this apply to everybody, or only to me? And what does holding this rule actually get me?

Quite often the answer to the last one is nothing except the guilt.

Replace it with a want or a will

The useful move is not to abandon your standards; it is to change the grammar.

I should exercise becomes I want to feel better, so I will walk twenty minutes after lunch. I should visit more becomes I will go every other Sunday and I will call on the weeks I do not.

Would like, want to, will, or choose to. Each of those keeps the value and removes the whip, and the striking thing is how much more often people actually do the thing once it is phrased that way.

And some shoulds should simply be dropped. I should enjoy my twenties. I should be a natural at this. I should not mind. Those are not standards; they are instructions about how to feel, and nobody can follow those.

Watch for the comparison versions

A large share of them are really about other people''s timelines.

I should own a house, be married, have been promoted, be settled by now. Those arrive from looking sideways, and the sideways view is always of other people''s edited highlights rather than their Tuesday afternoons.

The question worth putting in their place is what you want your life to contain, in your own words, this year. That is answerable. Being on schedule is not, because there is no schedule.

Notice the ones about feelings

The heaviest shoulds are often about emotion rather than behavior.

I should not be this upset. I should be grateful. I should have moved on. I should not be angry with someone who is dead.

Those add a second problem on top of the first: now you have the feeling, and the verdict about having it. And a feeling that is not allowed does not go away; it goes underground and comes out sideways.

The alternative is plain acknowledgment: I am angry, that makes sense given what happened, and I can decide what to do with it. The allowing is what lets the feeling move.

Keep the handful that are worth having

This is not an argument for having no standards at all.

A few rules are worth holding firmly: how you treat people, what you will not do, what you keep your word about. Those tend to feel steadying rather than crushing, which is a useful test.

If a rule makes you more careful, keep it. If it only makes you feel bad about yourself and changes nothing, it is doing no work, and it can go.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a therapist or a counselor if the guilt is constant rather than occasional, if the rules are strict enough that nothing you do ever counts, or if the voice saying them is harsh in a way you would never use with anyone else.

This is one of the most well-mapped areas in therapy: spotting a rule, testing it, and rewriting it is practical work that most people see results from within a handful of sessions. And if the weight of never being good enough has flattened your mood for weeks, mention that to a doctor too; it is common, treatable, and much easier to work on than it feels while the rules are still in charge.', array['CBT', 'Emotion Regulation']::text[], 0, '2026-10-15T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('doing-what-works-instead-of-being-right', 'Doing What Works Instead of Being Right', 'You are correct, and the conversation is going nowhere. A skill for the moments when insisting on the principle costs you the thing you actually wanted.', 'You are right. The charge really was wrong. He really did say he would do it. The appointment really was booked for Tuesday.

And you have been on the phone for forty minutes, or arguing in the kitchen for an hour, and you are no closer to the refund, the help, or the evening you wanted to have.

Being right and getting what you want are different goals

Most of us are taught that if we are right, pressing it is the correct thing to do. In practice that collapses two separate questions.

One is what is true. The other is what you want to come out of this. Sometimes pressing the first gets you the second, and sometimes it is exactly what stops you.

The skill is noticing which situation you are in, and choosing the one that matters more here. That is not giving in, and it is not pretending you were wrong. It is deciding where to spend your effort.

Name the actual goal first

Before the next sentence, work out what you want when this is over.

The parcel replaced. A peaceful dinner. Your father to accept the handrail. To get out of the shop in ten minutes. Your teenager to still talk to you next week.

Then look at whether what you are about to say moves you toward that or away from it. An awful lot of what people say in these moments is aimed at the record rather than at the result.

And be honest when the goal really is the principle. Sometimes it is: a complaint that should be on file, a line that needs saying out loud. Fine — then pursue that deliberately, in writing, to someone who can act, rather than by repeating yourself to someone who cannot.

What this looks like in practice

With a call center, the person on the phone did not make the policy. Getting them on your side works better than proving them wrong: I know this is not your doing, I am stuck, what are my options?

With a relative who will not accept help, winning the argument about whether they need it generally loses the next six months. Dropping the argument and making the help smaller gets further.

With a teenager, being right about the mess tends to cost you the conversation about the thing that actually matters.

With a partner mid-argument, the moment you start assembling evidence is usually the moment the evening is lost. Who said what three weeks ago is almost never the real subject.

The useful question in all of those: is this working?

Letting go of the last word

The hardest part is the final sentence, the one that proves the point. That sentence is where most of the damage lives, and it is nearly always optional.

Try leaving it unsaid. Not swallowing your view — you can say it once, plainly — but declining to say it a fourth time.

If you need something to do instead: ask a question, take a breath and name the goal to yourself, or say I think we see this differently and I would like to sort the practical bit.

And notice how quickly things move once somebody stops needing to be agreed with. Often the other person concedes the point later, when there is nothing to defend.

Where the urge comes from

If letting a wrong thing stand feels unbearable, that is usually about something older than the parcel.

People who were often blamed unfairly, or never believed, or made to apologize for things they did not do, tend to find being right enormously important, and for good reason. The need to establish the record was once protective.

Knowing that does not make it go away and it does change what you do with it. The question becomes: do I need this person to agree, or do I need the outcome? And sometimes: who can I tell about this afterward who will believe me, so I do not have to win it here?

Decide in advance where you will stop

This works much better planned than improvised.

Before a call, decide what a good result is and how long you will spend. Before a visit, pick the one or two things that genuinely matter and let the rest go visibly. Before a hard conversation, write the sentence you want to land in one line.

Then stop when you said you would, even if the other person is still wrong. Writing the complaint down, escalating it properly, or walking away are all real options and none of them require agreement.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a therapist or a counselor if arguments are taking over your relationships, if you cannot let things go even when it is costing you, or if the need to be believed is strong enough to keep you locked in conflicts with family, neighbors or an organization.

This is standard therapy ground and the skills for it are teachable rather than a matter of character. And if the anger and rumination around it are keeping you awake, or you are drinking to manage them, mention that to a doctor as well; both are common, treatable, and far easier to work on with help than alone.', array['DBT', 'Goals']::text[], 0, '2026-10-15T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
